ures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":194.32074207983192,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":215.45318,"r":222.57551000000004,"t":198.09758999999997},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/48","text":"A+D"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"B","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":215.92134957983194,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":201.7585,"r":207.61525,"t":225.24689999999998},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/49","text":"B"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Apoptosis Assays that Detect Membrane Asymmetry","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":618.3953888888889,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.3056,"r":472.37786000000017,"t":647.6025},"charspan":[0,47],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/50","text":"Apoptosis Assays that Detect Membrane Asymmetry"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":590.1697794117647,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.3056,"r":486.34159999999974,"t":612.3895},"charspan":[0,67],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/51","text":"Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit (A35137) provides a simple and fast method for detecting  apoptosis  with  dead-cell  discrimination  by  flow  cytometry.  The violet  ratiometric  membrane  asymmetry  probe,  F2N12S  (4ʹN , N -diethylamino-6-( N -dodecylN -methylN -(3-sulfopropyl))ammoniomethyl-3-hydroxyflavone, Figure 15.5.11), is a novel violet diode-excitable dye for the detection of membrane phospholipid asymmetry changes during apoptosis. This dye exhibits an excited-state intramolecular proton transfer (ESIPT) reaction, resulting in a dual fluorescence with two emission bands corresponding to 530 nm and 585 nm and producing a two-color ratiometric response to variations in surface charge. 59 This ratiometric probe is therefore a self-calibrating indicator of apoptotic transformation, which is independent of probe concentration, cell size and instrument variation, such as fluctuations of laser intensity or sensitivity of the detectors.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":424.69085591506575,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.2878240000001,"r":557.3350999999999,"t":589.2416999999999},"charspan":[0,995],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/52","text":"The Violet Ratiometric Membrane Asymmetry Probe/Dead Cell Apoptosis Kit (A35137) provides a simple and fast method for detecting  apoptosis  with  dead-cell  discrimination  by  flow  cytometry.  The violet  ratiometric  membrane  asymmetry  probe,  F2N12S  (4ʹN , N -diethylamino-6-( N -dodecylN -methylN -(3-sulfopropyl))ammoniomethyl-3-hydroxyflavone, Figure 15.5.11), is a novel violet diode-excitable dye for the detection of membrane phospholipid asymmetry changes during apoptosis. This dye exhibits an excited-state intramolecular proton transfer (ESIPT) reaction, resulting in a dual fluorescence with two emission bands corresponding to 530 nm and 585 nm and producing a two-color ratiometric response to variations in surface charge. 59 This ratiometric probe is therefore a self-calibrating indicator of apoptotic transformation, which is independent of probe concentration, cell size and instrument variation, such as fluctuations of laser intensity or sensitivity of the detectors."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Given that apoptosis modifies the surface charge of the outer leaflet of the plasma membrane, F2N12S can be used to monitor changes in membrane asymmetry that occur during apoptosis through a change in the relative intensity of the two emission bands of the dye 59 (Figure 15.5.12). The F2N12S-based apoptosis assay allows samples to be analyzed after a 5-minute incubation at room temperature and does not require special buffers or wash steps. This kit can be paired with other reagents such as MitoProbe™ DiIC1(5) or annexin V for multiparametric analysis of apoptosis and viability. The kit components, number of assays and assay principles are summarized in Table 15.4.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":314.69085591506575,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.3035,"r":557.3066999999999,"t":424.24170000000004},"charspan":[0,674],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/53","text":"Given that apoptosis modifies the surface charge of the outer leaflet of the plasma membrane, F2N12S can be used to monitor changes in membrane asymmetry that occur during apoptosis through a change in the relative intensity of the two emission bands of the dye 59 (Figure 15.5.12). The F2N12S-based apoptosis assay allows samples to be analyzed after a 5-minute incubation at room temperature and does not require special buffers or wash steps. This kit can be paired with other reagents such as MitoProbe™ DiIC1(5) or annexin V for multiparametric analysis of apoptosis and viability. The kit components, number of assays and assay principles are summarized in Table 15.4."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Annexin V Conjugates","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":293.1737794117647,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.3055,"r":399.28600000000006,"t":304.3895},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/54","text":"Annexin V Conjugates"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The human vascular anticoagulant annexin V is a 35-36 kilodalton, Ca 2+ -dependent phospholipid-binding protein that has a high affinity for the anionic phospholipid phosphatidylserine (PS). In normal","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":259.69085591506575,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.308,"r":557.2056000000006,"t":292.24170000000004},"charspan":[0,200],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/55","text":"The human vascular anticoagulant annexin V is a 35-36 kilodalton, Ca 2+ -dependent phospholipid-binding protein that has a high affinity for the anionic phospholipid phosphatidylserine (PS). In normal"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"F2N12S ratio 585 nm/530 nm","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":131.15103311974792,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":336.5106,"r":410.1578250000003,"t":136.81630500000006},"charspan":[0,26],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/56","text":"F2N12S ratio 585 nm/530 nm"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"fluorescence x 1,000","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":125.07664061974788,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":347.9650125,"r":398.7034125,"t":130.7419125},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/57","text":"fluorescence x 1,000"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"SYTOX ®  AADvanced ™  fluorescence","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":143.5669868,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":305.7943506,"r":311.6842668802521,"t":229.99258544621853},"charspan":[0,34],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/58","text":"SYTOX ®  AADvanced ™  fluorescence"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10 1","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":145.28459242647057,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":317.2625,"r":323.36973520000004,"t":149.69091500000002},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/59","text":"10 1"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10 2","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":164.9737924264706,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":317.458,"r":323.5652352,"t":169.38011499999993},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/60","text":"10 2"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10 3","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/3"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":184.66209242647062,"coord_origin":"B